---
const steps = [
  {
    n: "01",
    title: "Retain",
    body: "Write durable facts, preferences, and decisions. Auto-tier or set semantic / working explicitly. Similar facts merge; clear contradictions supersede.",
  },
  {
    n: "02",
    title: "Recall",
    body: "Retrieve ranked context for the current task. Configurable multi-factor score: FTS, importance, decay, tier, recency, entity.",
  },
  {
    n: "03",
    title: "Reflect",
    body: "Session write-back consolidates decisions and preferences. Heuristic extract by default; optional LLM reflect behind a flag.",
  },
  {
    n: "04",
    title: "Prune & promote",
    body: "Scores and caps keep the store clean. Multi-pass merge on prune. Patterns can promote to procedural skills with quality gates.",
  },
];
---

<section class="section-pad border-0 bg-white" id="how" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
  <div class="container-content">
    <div class="max-w-2xl">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-4">How it works</p>
      <h2
        id="how-heading"
        class="font-display text-[clamp(1.75rem,3.5vw,2.5rem)] font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-black"
      >
        A disciplined write path.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-500">
        Useful agent memory is not a bigger store. It is hierarchical retention
        plus aggressive consolidation.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ol class="mt-10 grid gap-3 sm:mt-14 sm:grid-cols-2 sm:gap-4 lg:grid-cols-4">
      {
        steps.map((step) => (
          <li class="panel relative p-5 sm:p-6">
            <span class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">{step.n}</span>
            <h3 class="mt-3 font-display text-base font-semibold tracking-tight text-black sm:mt-4">
              {step.title}
            </h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-500">{step.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>
